cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework and common state
  representation for describing how a shared multidimensional real-resource
  world K, actor-specific access and usability views K_i, and actor-specific
  subjective evaluation and expectation states P_i condition actor-side
  processes and events A_i. Resource in VFT is not limited to physical goods;
  externally instantiated and usable, exercisable, or referenceable information,
  rights, qualifications, contracts, institutional conditions, and financial
  claims may also be represented in K. K_i is not required to be a
  set-theoretic subset of K; it is a derived actor-relative access, usability,
  exercisability, or referenceability view of the shared resource world.
  P_i is multidimensional and may contain conceptually distinct belief-like,
  evaluative or preference-like, and relational or trust-like components
  without introducing separate Core primitives. For another actor a, P_j(a)
  denotes actor j's evaluation, belief, credit assessment, or expectation about
  a, rather than a state held by a. Delta K(S,tau) is reserved for a
  specification-indexed state or resource change descriptor. Gross production,
  consumption, transaction volume, transformation, and other interval activity
  statistics are represented separately by projection-local derived path
  functionals such as H_S(tau)=h_S((K_t)_{t in tau},A_tau). Where an expectation
  is defined, E_i[Delta K(S,tau)] is shorthand for an expectation after a
  quantitative representation q_S:D_S->V_S; candidate-action comparisons may
  use action-contingent forecasts such as E_i[Delta K(S,tau)|A=a,I_i]. Planned
  or chosen resource changes are distinct from forecasts and may be represented
  by projection-local variables such as x_i. Micro and macro observations refer
  to the same common K and K transitions under different observation or
  accounting specifications S; a universal micro-to-macro aggregation map is
  not required by the Core. Above this ontology, VFT adopts three management
  rationality axioms for decision-making: resource-realization, activity-flow,
  and P-downside. They correspond respectively to resource-outcome realization,
  continuity or expansion of activity processes, and prevention of P-side
  deterioration that threatens system viability. The three axioms are not
  ontological actor types and are not fixed one-to-one assignments. Any actor
  may consider all three, while their priority, weight, or division varies with
  context, role, institution, organizational structure, and time horizon. A
  single autarkic actor may bear all three. At a coarse national-macro level in
  capitalist institutions, individuals may be resource-realization-heavy,
  firms activity-flow-heavy, and the state P-downside-heavy, without excluding
  the other rationalities from any actor. Concrete objective functions,
  priorities, constraints, and decision rules are projection-specific rather
  than Core primitives. Utility and profit are therefore treated as possible
  evaluation functions or performance indicators used in concrete decisions,
  not as specializations of the axioms themselves. Economic budget feasibility,
  inter-agent compatibility, market-equilibrium conditions, accounting
  identities, surplus, decentralized adjustment, and reputation- or
  credit-economy models remain domain-specific projection constructs rather
  than universal consequences of the VFT Core. VFT does not fix a universal
  probability measure, expectation-formation equation, deterministic
  action-selection function, state-transition law, compatibility condition,
  market-equilibrium condition, quantitative objective, rationality weighting
  rule, or aggregation law.
